speechtext:
The House went into Committee on the following proposed resolution:
That the following, being the findings of the Judge of the Exchequer Court made on the 15th day of March, A.D., 1916, under the provisions of section 49a of the Indian Act as enacted by the Statutes of 1911, chapter fourteen, be approved:-
"1. That it is expedient, having regard to the interest of the public and of the Indians located on the small Sydney Reserve in the City of Sydney, in the Province of Nova Scotia, that the said Indians should be removed from such Reserve;
"2. That the compensation fixed by the said Judge should be paid respectively to the individual Indians of the band for the special loss or damages sustained by them in respect of their buildings or improvements upon the Reserve, or an adjustment be made for their claims in respect thereof, and a suitable new Reserve be obtained for them before they be removed from or disturbed in the possession of the present Reserve;
"3. That the Indians should, on their removal be treated with great consideration and kindness and that such removal should be made quietly, without undue haste, trouble or inconvenience to the Indians after the selection of a new Reserve and the payment of compensation for their buildings or improvements."-Mr. Roche.
(Mr Dugald Stewart in the Chair.)
